+
+## a64 — disconnect reason found, raw link DIAG record, canary off (BUILT, NOT INSTALLED)
+
+Commits: 648f8b3 (canary switch), 564073e (DIAG record and parser), this ledger row.
+
+1) RAM canary off. The test-26 fill of 0x01C72000-0x01C74000 is now behind `X0X_RAM_CANARY` (default 0). The pool ends at 0x01C73000 in this build, so the fill wrote over the last 4 KiB of live .pool. With 0, the code clears fm1_can.armed, because .ble_ram keeps data after a reset. Set it to 1 only for a test build with 8 KiB of pool free.
+2) Disconnect reason. The disassembly of btstack_task (hci_vendor.c:1009 and 1014 in ctl.elf) shows that JieLi gives each HCI event to the handlers with size = parameter length + 1. That is one byte short. So Disconnection Complete arrives with size 5, but its reason is at p[5]. The SDK apps also read packet[5]. a61 read p[5] only when size >= 6, and a63 read p[size-1] (the handle high byte). Both gave 0x00. a64 reads p[5]. d_disc0-3 keep the a61 layout, with the real reason in bits 0-7.
+3) DIAG record in the image. The image writes 14 words into the mailbox c2h ring pads (c2h.pad0[7], c2h.pad1[7]). Only the image writes them, and the app does not use them. Thus the app gets no new RAM and no new code. The record has the raw first 8 bytes of the last 2 disconnects, the event size, who asked for the end (our BLE_CMD_DISCONNECT or not), the notify state, the setup flags, and the ms from the connection to the disconnect. It also has the role, peer address type, interval, latency and supervision timeout of the last 2 connections, and the last 4 HCI event codes. hx_sm moved to piece 2b to give piece 1 the room.
+4) tools/ble_autotest.py --diag-only now prints the setup ring for each connection, the parameter request state (d_poll_gap high bits), d_updates, the 4 disconnect words with the reason in words, the poll gap, the link latency and timeout, and the a64 record. It finds the mailbox in BLERAM by its magic (0x01C7C480 in this build), or takes --mbox.
+
+No behaviour change. Pool, MTU, queues, sample slots, audio, connection parameters and advertising are the same as a63.
+
+Hypotheses for the iPhone drop before the first ATT, ranked by the evidence (a62b and a63 DIAG):
+1. The link layer loses the link in the first connection events (supervision timeout 0x08 or failed to establish 0x3E). Evidence: no MTU exchange in any of 4 (a63) and 11 (a62b) links, and iOS always sends the MTU request first. The only LE subevent in d_evlog is 0x01 (connection complete). The link has interval 26.25 ms, latency 0, timeout 720 ms. iOS connects again at once (4 links in one test). a64 shows it: reason 0x08 or 0x3E with a time under about 1 s.
+2. The link stays up but ACL data from the iPhone does not reach the host (a regression in the ACL path to btstack). Evidence: the user saw the iPhone hold the link for about 2 min (a61, a62b) with rx 0. a64 shows it: reason 0x13 (the central ended it) after about 30 s or more. Test: connect the Mac. If the Mac also shows no MTU step, the ACL path is broken for all centrals.
+3. iOS ends the link after an LL control procedure (feature or version exchange). Our controller offers only LE encryption (same as the SDK default). Evidence: weak. a64 shows reason 0x1A, 0x22 or 0x13 soon after the connection.
+4. Core 1 too slow. Evidence against: d_poll_gap 1 ms (a62b), queue full 0, heap failed 0. Interrupt latency is not measured.
+5. Pairing or encryption. Evidence against: pairs 0, failed pairs 0, no encryption event.
+6. Advertising data, GATT table or address. Evidence against: the advertising data (flags 0x06 and the BLE-MIDI 128-bit UUID) and the GATT table are byte for byte the same as in the working SMK-37 Oscuro firmware (reference only, not in git). The only difference is the write-with-response flag on the MIDI I/O value (ours 0x031E, Oscuro 0x0316), which iOS does not use before the MTU step. The address is random static with the top bits 11.
+Reference compare (SMK-37 Oscuro, CRC-32 4f326e15): same JieLi btctrler and btstack families. It has a "gap send conn param" log string that our btstack.a does not have. Its advertising interval, address type and controller settings are in code and were not found without a full disassembly.
+
+Build: app code area 552672 B, 144 B free. App RAM .data+.bss 98304 of 98304 B (0 B free). Pool 339968 of 344064 B (4096 B free, the limit). ctl.img piece 1 65456 of 65536 B (80 B free), piece 2 49136 of 49152 B (2a 4 B free, 2b 16 B free). Image RAM 27176 of 27264 B, RAM 2 unchanged. The Mac build of ctl.img gives the same CRCs as the box build. The Mac app build fails (XIP overflow 192 B). The probable cause is the font generator: the Mac Pillow has no libraqm, the box Pillow has it. Build the app on the box.
+Tests: tests/ble_test 25157 checks, 0 failed. tests/run_tests.sh on the box: 64 ok, 3 not run (drum909, drum808, bass303: the reference sources ../schwung-* are not on the box).
+Artifacts (Mac ~/Documents/x0x37): a64-x0x-smk37-elite.fwsc (crc32 5ec8b21a, 651396 B), a64-ctl.img (crc32 e9c05144, 114672 B; piece1 d45e7f2e, piece2 738e4296), a64.bundle. NOT installed.
